<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kōzuke Province</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kozuke Province</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kōzuke Province was an old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanto-region">Kanto region</a>, now Gunma Prefecture. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> it was contested by the Uesugi, Takeda and Later Hōjō, and under the Tokugawa it was divided among several domains, including Maebashi and Takasaki. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> industry, later symbolised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomioka-silk-mill">Tomioka Silk Mill</a> of 1872, made it important in Japan's modernisation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kozuke-province.jpg" alt="Kōzuke Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Kozuke.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
